    <h1>The File Is the Grammar: Provenance-Preserving Question Answering o=
ver a Personal Corpus, without a Language Model</h1>
    <p class=3D"byline">antfriend</p>
    <dl class=3D"meta">
      <dt>Subjects</dt><dd>Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Int=
elligence (cs.AI)</dd>
      <dt>Keywords</dt><dd>personal knowledge graph, self-describing data, =
defeasible inheritance, attribution, controlled natural language, multiling=
ual grammars</dd>
      <dt>Comments</dt><dd>Artifact: <code>personal_grammar/</code> (index.=
html, personal_grammar_ttdb.md, TTG-RFC-0001=E2=80=930003), CC0. Benchmark =
data: bAbI v1.2, CC BY 3.0.</dd>
    </dl>
    <div class=3D"abstract">
      <p class=3D"label">Abstract</p>
      <p>Interfaces built on large language models answer in fluent text wh=
ose sources are opaque. We present Personal Grammar, a question-answering s=
ystem over one person's own words that can say only three kinds of thing: w=
hat the person said, what follows from it along a chain it shows, and that =
the person has said both. Sentences are parsed into subject=E2=80=93vector=
=E2=80=93object percepts, consolidated per episode into beliefs by evidence=
 counting, and queried with defeasible IS-A inheritance; contradictions are=
 kept as a fourth truth value rather than resolved.</p>
      <p>The system's distinguishing property is architectural. A single pl=
ain-text file is at once the data, the schema that data instantiates, the g=
rammar that reads it, and a compressed specification of the interpreter, an=
d the interpreter contains no word of any natural language. We test this by=
 substitution. Removing the grammar leaves the parser only word order. A Sp=
anish grammar sits beside the English one and answers Spanish questions fro=
m English sayings, with no Spanish in the interpreter. On bAbI task 15 (bas=
ic deduction), applying one fixed letter bijection to both the grammar and =
the data leaves all 1,000 question outcomes unchanged, while a negative con=
trol scores 0%.</p>
      <p>With one declared line of grammar data the system answers all 1,00=
0 test questions correctly and cites exactly the gold supporting facts as i=
ts grounds, at about 0.2 ms per question with no trained parameters. Withou=
t that line it answers none, and it fails bAbI task 1 for reasons we trace =
to its design. We discuss the narrow coverage this buys and position the ap=
proach as a self-describing, local-first alternative where attribution matt=
ers more than breadth.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main>
    <section aria-labelledby=3D"claims">
      <h2 id=3D"claims">What the paper claims</h2>
      <p class=3D"lede">None of the parts is new on its own. The contributi=
on is the combination, and properties of it that can be checked.</p>
      <ol class=3D"contrib">
        <li><strong>A self-describing artifact.</strong> Instances, schema,=
 grammar and interpreter specification share one record format, and conform=
ance tests hold the prose specification to the running code.</li>
        <li><strong>Grammar as data, tested three ways:</strong> deletion, =
a second language, and a letter permutation that must leave every outcome u=
nchanged.</li>
        <li><strong>Attributable answers by construction.</strong> Grounds =
are typed said, inferred or contested and printed differently; on bAbI task=
 15 the cited grounds equal the gold supporting facts on every question.</l=
i>
        <li><strong>Deterministic consolidation.</strong> Evidence is count=
ed per episode, confidence is a Beta-mean (Laplace) estimate, and contradic=
tions are kept as Belnap's <em>both</em>.</li>
        <li><strong>Several languages, one relational algebra.</strong> Sen=
tences choose a grammar; later languages borrow the first one's vectors. Me=
rging the lexicons instead breaks 2 of 15 English parses.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby=3D"results">
      <h2 id=3D"results">First results</h2>
      <div class=3D"tablewrap">
        <table>
